at time is not a mere container of events but an a priori form through which the undetermined becomes determinate. His Kantian critique is thus characterized as a decisive intervention in epistemology and the philosophy of mind, shaping notions of consciousness, reason, and the limits of knowledge.\n\nSeveral descriptions emphasize Kant’s use of the term transcendental and his exploration of time—sometimes described in provocative terms as “out of joint” or as presenting itself in a “pure state”—and note that Kant’s ideas are invoked to frame broader theoretical concepts, including the time-image in cinema theory. Related notes trace Kant’s influence in discussions of memory, reminiscence, imagination, and faculties within a framework that is frequently labeled transcendental empiricism, often alongside Platonic imagery about the operation of memory across domains of experience.\n\nMoreover, Kant is depicted as positing a two-tier structure of reality, described in commentary as two distinct “floors” whose autonomy is respected: the upper floor being a realm that can be emptied or inhabited, reflecting a world-division concept that aligns with Kant’s famous distinction between phenomena (the world as it appears to us) and noumena (things in themselves). This two-floor schema is tied to a broader account of how the ego relates to reality, including notions of passive receptivity and active synthesis, and the interplay between the senses and a priori forms.\n\nIn a detailed account of Kantian psychology and aesthetics, Kant’s notion of the passive self as receptivity—where sensations are received already formed and then ordered by the a priori forms of space and time—underpins the distinction between passive and active synthesis. The ego’s unity, the test of reality, and the division of the aesthetic into an objective element (space) and a subjective element (pleasure and pain) are highlighted as central to understanding how subjects engage with objects, whether in ac